\fs44 9. Describe the process of troubleshooting network connectivity issues.\par
\b\fs40 ans:1. Identify the Problem:\par
\fs36 ->\b0  Define the problem (symptoms, error messages) and Identify affected devices/network segments.\par
\b -> \b0 Collect network documentation (topology, configs) and Determine user reports and impact.\par
\b\fs40 2. Check Physical Connections:\par
->\b0\fs36 Verify that all cables (Ethernet, fiber optics) are securely connected.\par
\b\fs40 ->\b0\fs36 Inspect for any visible damage to cables or ports.\b\fs40\par
->\b0\fs36 Ensure that switches, routers, and modems are powered on and functioning properly.\par
\b\fs40 3. Verify Device Configuration:\par
\fs36 ->\b0 Check the network settings on the affected device (IP address, subnet mask, gateway).\par
\b ->\b0 Ensure the device is configured to obtain an IP address automatically (if using DHCP) or check static settings.\par
\b ->\b0 Confirm that the correct SSID and password are used for Wi-Fi connections.\par
\b\fs44 4. Ping Tests:\fs36\par
->\b0 Local Ping: Start by pinging the local gateway (e.g., router) to check for local network issues.\par
\b ->\b0 External Ping: Ping a well-known external address to verify internet connectivity.\par
\b\fs44 5. Check for Network Interference:\b0\fs36\par
\b ->\b0 Wi-Fi Signal Strength: For wireless connections, check signal strength and interference from other devices or networks.\par
\b ->\b0 Change Channels: If interference is suspected, try changing the Wi-Fi channel in the router settings.\par
\b\fs44 6. Inspect Network Hardware:\b0\fs36\par
\b ->\b0 Reboot Devices: Restart the router, modem, and affected devices to clear temporary issues.\par
\b ->\b0 Firmware Updates: Check for and apply firmware updates to networking equipment.\par
\b\fs44 7. Examine Network Configuration:\par
\fs36 ->\b0 Firewall Settings: Ensure that firewall settings aren\rquote t blocking the connection.\par
\b ->\b0 Access Control: Check if MAC filtering or access control lists (ACLs) are preventing access.\par
\b\fs44 8. Run Network Diagnostic Tools:\par
\fs36 ->\b0 Use built-in tools like Windows Network Troubleshooter or command-line utilities like traceroute or nslookup to further diagnose issues.\par
\b\fs44 9. Check for ISP Issues:\b0\fs36\par
\b ->\b0 Outages: Verify with the Internet Service Provider (ISP) if there are any outages or service disruptions.\par
\b ->\b0 Connection Type: Ensure that the connection type (e.g., DSL, fiber) is functioning properly.\par
\b\fs44 10. Document Findings:\b0\fs36\par
\b ->\b0 Keep a log of what was tested, results, and any changes made to help in future troubleshooting or if escalation is needed.\par
\b\fs44 11. Seek Additional Help:\par
\fs36 ->\b0 If the problem persists, escalate the issue to a higher-level technical support team or consult online resources/forums for assistance.\par
